## Response Structure

With `show-details=always`:
```json
{
  "status": "UP",
  "components": {
    "db": { "status": "UP", "details": { "database": "PostgreSQL", "validationQuery": "isValid()" } },
    "diskSpace": { "status": "UP", "details": { "total": 499963174912, "free": 91000000000 } },
    "redis": { "status": "UP", "details": { "version": "7.0.0" } }
  }
}
```

## Built-in Health Indicators

Auto-configured when the relevant dependency is present: `DataSourceHealthIndicator`, `DiskSpaceHealthIndicator`, `RedisHealthIndicator`, `RabbitHealthIndicator`, `CassandraHealthIndicator`.

## Custom HealthIndicator

```java
@Component
public class PaymentServiceHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {
    @Override
    public Health health() {
        boolean reachable = pingPaymentService();
        return reachable
            ? Health.up().withDetail("latency", "12ms").build()
            : Health.down().withDetail("reason", "timeout").build();
    }
}
```

## Kubernetes Probes

```properties
management.endpoint.health.probes.enabled=true
# /actuator/health/liveness  — is the app alive? (restart if DOWN)
# /actuator/health/readiness — is the app ready for traffic? (remove from service if DOWN)
```

## Production Reality

A common mistake is making health checks too strict. If your payment service dependency is temporarily down, should your entire app report `DOWN`? Usually not — use a separate composite health group or mark non-critical indicators as warning-level only.

## Basic Implementation

```java
@Component
public class ExternalApiHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    private final RestTemplate restTemplate;

    public ExternalApiHealthIndicator(RestTemplate restTemplate) {
        this.restTemplate = restTemplate;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        try {
            ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate
                .getForEntity("https://api.example.com/ping", String.class);
            if (response.getStatusCode().is2xxSuccessful()) {
                return Health.up()
                    .withDetail("status", response.getStatusCode().value())
                    .build();
            }
            return Health.down()
                .withDetail("status", response.getStatusCode().value())
                .build();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Never throw — always return Health.down()
            return Health.down()
                .withDetail("error", e.getMessage())
                .build();
        }
    }
}
```

## Naming in Health Response

```
Bean: externalApiHealthIndicator
Key in /actuator/health: externalApi
```

## Excluding Non-Critical Indicators from Overall Status

```properties
# Create a health group that excludes non-critical indicators
management.endpoint.health.group.readiness.include=db,diskSpace
# External API health won't pull the whole app DOWN
```

## Production Reality

Always catch exceptions in your `health()` method and return `Health.down()` instead of letting them propagate. An uncaught exception in a health indicator causes `/actuator/health` to return a 500 error, which kills Kubernetes liveness probes and triggers unnecessary pod restarts.

## Architecture

```
Your Code → MeterRegistry (Micrometer) → Backend Registry → Monitoring System
                                          ↓
                              PrometheusMeterRegistry → /actuator/prometheus
                              DatadogMeterRegistry    → Datadog API
                              CloudWatchMeterRegistry → AWS CloudWatch
```

## Auto-Configured Metrics

Just by adding Actuator, you get:
- `jvm.memory.used` / `jvm.memory.max`
- `jvm.gc.pause` (GC duration)
- `jvm.threads.live`
- `http.server.requests` (count, latency, status)
- `process.cpu.usage`
- `hikaricp.connections.*` (if using HikariCP)

## Custom Metrics

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final Counter orderCounter;
    private final Timer orderProcessingTimer;

    public OrderService(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.orderCounter = registry.counter("orders.created", "type", "online");
        this.orderProcessingTimer = registry.timer("order.processing.duration");
    }

    public Order createOrder(OrderRequest request) {
        return orderProcessingTimer.record(() -> {
            orderCounter.increment();
            return processOrder(request);
        });
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Tag your metrics well from day one. Tags like `service`, `region`, `version` let you filter and aggregate in your monitoring system. A metric without useful tags is hard to act on when something goes wrong at 2am.

## Counter

```java
Counter errorCounter = registry.counter("payment.errors", "type", "timeout");
errorCounter.increment();
// or increment by amount:
errorCounter.increment(3.0);
```

## Gauge

```java
// Gauge a queue size
Queue<Order> pendingOrders = new LinkedList<>();
Gauge.builder("orders.pending", pendingOrders, Queue::size)
     .description("Number of orders awaiting processing")
     .register(registry);
// Gauge reads pendingOrders.size() automatically
```

## Timer

```java
Timer timer = Timer.builder("db.query.duration")
    .description("Database query execution time")
    .tags("query", "find_user")
    .register(registry);

// Manual timing
Timer.Sample sample = Timer.start(registry);
User user = userRepository.findById(id);
sample.stop(timer);

// Or with lambda
timer.record(() -> userRepository.findById(id));
```

## @Timed Annotation

```java
@Service
public class UserService {
    @Timed(value = "user.service.findById", description = "Find user by ID")
    public User findById(Long id) {
        return userRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    }
}
```

Requires `TimedAspect` bean:
```java
@Bean
public TimedAspect timedAspect(MeterRegistry registry) {
    return new TimedAspect(registry);
}
```

## Production Reality

Keep metric names consistent across services — use dots as separators (`payment.errors`), not underscores or camelCase. Inconsistent naming forces everyone to remember which service uses which convention when writing Grafana queries.

## Sample Response Structure

```json
{
  "contexts": {
    "application": {
      "mappings": {
        "dispatcherServlets": {
          "dispatcherServlet": [
            {
              "handler": "public com.example.UserController.getUser(Long)",
              "predicate": "{GET /api/users/{id}, produces [application/json]}",
              "details": {
                "handlerMethod": {
                  "className": "com.example.UserController",
                  "name": "getUser"
                },
                "requestMappingConditions": {
                  "methods": ["GET"],
                  "patterns": ["/api/users/{id}"]
                }
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Enabling

```properties
management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=mappings
```

## What's Included

- Your `@Controller` / `@RestController` methods
- Actuator endpoint paths
- `/error` error handler
- Static resource handlers (`/static/**`, `/webjars/**`)
- Any manually registered `RequestMappingHandlerMapping` entries

## Production Reality

Useful during development and debugging. In production, this endpoint reveals your full API surface — consider keeping it on the internal management port only. A security auditor can use this to enumerate all your endpoints without having to read your source code.
